@ Index:   Original Binet graphing program
@ Author:  Jaco Mostert
@ Created: unknown publication date
@ Version: 1.0
@ Tested:  Fills 101 samples; PLSTAT plots the Binet function, (-1.3, -1.02) to (5.3, 1.02).
@ Input:   none; PLSTAT after the run
@ Output:  Binet plot, (-1.3, -1.02) to (5.3, 1.02); closed curves for negative x
@
@ Samples a closed form from -2 to 5 and accumulate each value.
@ R.00 counts 101 steps down from 101.  The argument is
@
@   n = 7*(R.01 - R.00)/(R.01 - 1) - 2
@
@ CALCLP1 returns (φ^n - (-φ)^(-X))/√5, where X is the stack
@ register present when Binet is called.  A cleared stack makes the
@ second term 1. Σ+ takes the real part as x and the imaginary
@ part as y. STOCFG and STOS save the configuration and the stack;
@ the matching recalls put them back.  CLΣ clears the sample.
LBL ‘Binet1’
  LocR 11
  STOCFG R.02       ; put back by RCLCFG
  STOS R.03         ; put back by RCLS
  CLΣ
  101               ; 101 samples, n from -2 to 5
  STO R.00
  STO R.01

  LBL ‘M1’
    RCL R.01
    RCL R.00
    -
    RCL R.01
    XEQ ‘CALCLP1’
    Σ+              ; x is the real part, y the imaginary part
    DSZ R.00
      GTO ‘M1’
    RCLS R.03       ; stack from before the sample
    RCLCFG R.02     ; configuration from before the sample
    RTN

  LBL ‘CALCLP1’
    DECR X
    ÷
    7
    ×
    2
    -
    ENTER
    φ
    𝑥⇄𝑦
    𝑦ˣ
    𝑥⇄𝑦
    φ
    CHS
    𝑥⇄𝑦
    CHS
    𝑦ˣ
    -
    5
    √𝑥
    ÷
    ENTER
    Im
    𝑥⇄𝑦
    Re
    RTN
.END.
